## What changed

**`src/roman.ts`**
- `toRoman(n)`: greedy match against a descending value table `[1000 M, 900 CM, 500 D, 400 CD, 100 C, 90 XC, 50 L, 40 XL, 10 X, 9 IX, 5 V, 4 IV, 1 I]`, so the subtractive pairs come out naturally. Rejects non-integers and anything outside 1–3999 with a `RangeError` whose message includes the rejected value.
- `fromRoman(text)`: validates against the canonical grammar `^(M{0,3})(CM|CD|D?C{0,3})(XC|XL|L?X{0,3})(IX|IV|V?I{0,3})$` after uppercasing (so either case is accepted, and the empty string fails since it must match at least one symbol), then sums the tokens. Anything non-standard — `IIII`, `VX`, `IC`, `MMMM`, `ABC`, whitespace, etc. — throws a `SyntaxError` naming the input via `JSON.stringify` so empty/whitespace strings stay visible in the message.

**`src/roman.test.ts`** (new, `bun:test`): canonical-value table (I, IV, IX, XLIV, CDXLIV, MCMXCIV, 3999 …); a full loop over 1–3999 asserting every output matches the grammar and round-trips; RangeError cases (`0, -1, 4000, 1e9, NaN, ±Infinity, 1.5`); case-insensitivity; SyntaxError cases including the ones you listed plus `IL`, `XM`, `VIV`, `DD`, `MCMC`, leading/trailing/embedded whitespace, and `Ⅻ`.

